WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.210
<v ->So next, the second part of this Route 53 lecture</v>

2
00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:05.370
...so we have Hosted Zones and this

3
00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:07.130
is a container of records to define

4
00:00:07.130 --> 00:00:08.500
how to route traffic to a domain

5
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:10.550
and its subdomains and we have two kinds.

6
00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:13.100
We have the Public Hosted Zones, to allow

7
00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:16.060
the internet to resolve them for public domains,

8
00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:17.720
but also we get the Private Hosted Zones

9
00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:19.940
and these can only be resolved from

10
00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:23.780
within your VPC and you can define private URL's.

11
00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:25.190
So, the difference is that the

12
00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:27.420
Public Hosted Zone is accessible publicly

13
00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:29.099
and you can have the target being

14
00:00:29.099 --> 00:00:31.120
the Public IP of an EC2 Instance,

15
00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:33.540
the Public IP of an Application Load Balancer

16
00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:35.740
or a CloudFront distribution or

17
00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:38.140
an S3 Bucket website for example.

18
00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:39.350
But in the Private Hosted Zone,

19
00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:41.360
it's used from within your VPC

20
00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:43.360
and it's very helpful to link to

21
00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:46.710
the Private IP of your EC2 Instances

22
00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:49.590
or the Private IP of your Database Instances

23
00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:52.030
and so on. Other good things to know

24
00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:54.720
for Route 53 is that in case you have a

25
00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:57.000
Private Hosted Zone with a Private DNS

26
00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.440
then in your VPC you must enable two settings

27
00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:01.537
called the enableDnsHostnames

28
00:01:01.537 --> 00:01:02.370
and enable DnsSupport.

29
00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:08.390
Also, on Route 53 you get support for the

30
00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:10.990
DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC)

31
00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:12.450
and it's a protocol for securing

32
00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:14.580
DNS traffic to verify the DNS

33
00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:17.410
data integrity and origin. It helps you

34
00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:19.730
to be protected against Man in the Middle (MITM)

35
00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:22.810
attacks on the DNSSEC. And Route 53

36
00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:24.941
has support for both DNSSEC for

37
00:01:24.941 --> 00:01:28.330
Domain Registration and also DNSSEC Signing.

38
00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:31.280
It only works with Public Hosted Zones.

39
00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:33.660
Finally, you can use Route 53 with a

40
00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:36.170
3rd Registrar in which case you buy the domain

41
00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:38.230
outside of AWS but you still use

42
00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:40.950
Route 53 as your DNS provider.

43
00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:42.360
For this, you just update the

44
00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:44.640
NS records on the Registrar and

45
00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.737
you appoint them to AWS.

46
00:01:47.943 --> 00:01:50.190
Onto Health Checks, so you get Public

47
00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:52.980
Health Checks on Route 53. That means that

48
00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:55.420
for example, if you have two ALB's in two

49
00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:57.820
distinct regions then you can set up two

50
00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:01.060
Health Checks to check the health of these ALB.

51
00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:03.600
And you use the Health Check,

52
00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.300
to link them to a specific DNS record

53
00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:09.470
and this gives you automatic DNS Failover.

54
00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:11.060
So, you have three kind of Health Checks.

55
00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:12.790
You get Health Checks that will monitor

56
00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:15.490
an endpoint health so: application,

57
00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:18.310
server or maybe another AWS resource.

58
00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:20.090
You get Health Checks that will monitor

59
00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:21.100
other Health Checks and they are

60
00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:23.240
called Calculated Health Checks.

61
00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:24.970
And you get Health Checks that monitor

62
00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:26.800
CloudWatch Alarms, which gives

63
00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:28.130
you full control because you do

64
00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:30.150
whatever you want with a CloudWatch alarm.

65
00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:31.336
So you can monitor, for example,

66
00:02:31.336 --> 00:02:33.620
throttles of DynamoDB alarms on

67
00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.950
RDS or any custom metrics which

68
00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:38.210
is very helpful for private resources.

69
00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:39.330
Now all of these Health Checks

70
00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:41.260
will publish also CloudWatch metrics

71
00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:43.280
if you ever need to. So as we said

72
00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:45.100
we have Calculated Health Checks

73
00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:47.000
which is to combine Health Checks

74
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.620
into a Parent Health Check and

75
00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:51.120
for this you can use the OR, AND,

76
00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:53.050
or NOT condition. You can monitor

77
00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:56.800
up to 256 Children Health Checks

78
00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:58.760
in one Parent Health Check and

79
00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:01.350
you specify how many of the Child

80
00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:03.140
Health Checks need to pass to make

81
00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:05.080
the Parent Health pass as well.

82
00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:07.010
So the use case of a Calculated

83
00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:08.870
Health Check is to perform maintenance

84
00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:10.690
on your website without causing, for

85
00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:13.810
example, all the Health Checks to fail.

86
00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:15.500
You can also monitor an Endpoint

87
00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:17.360
using Health Checks. So, we can

88
00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:21.210
monitor Public Endpoints and Health Checkers

89
00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:23.680
all around the world will do HFTP request

90
00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:26.720
to the health route that you set, okay?

91
00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:28.350
And when you do set something up

92
00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:29.960
you get about fifteen Health Checkers

93
00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:31.190
that will check your Endpoint health

94
00:03:31.190 --> 00:03:33.340
so it needs to be a Public Endpoint, of course.

95
00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:34.650
And then the Health Checks are

96
00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:36.310
going to pass only if the Endpoint

97
00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:38.381
is going to respond with the 200's

98
00:03:38.381 --> 00:03:41.530
or the 300's status codes. Now there's

99
00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:43.030
a cool feature that you need to know about

100
00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:44.680
that is coming up in the exam.

101
00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:45.940
Is that the health check can be

102
00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:48.430
set up to fail or pass based on

103
00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:51.270
the value returned by the Endpoint

104
00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:53.260
that you are checking. And if in the

105
00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:56.167
first 5120 bytes of the response

106
00:03:56.167 --> 00:03:58.320
you have some specific text that

107
00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:00.460
you specify then you make the Health

108
00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:02.063
Check pass or else the Health Check

109
00:04:02.063 --> 00:04:03.693
does not pass.

110
00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:08.130
Okay, so how do we do Health Checks of stuff in

111
00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:10.530
Private Hosted Zones? Well, because

112
00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:11.470
the Health Checkers are outside

113
00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:14.040
the VPC they cannot access Private Endpoints.

114
00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:17.430
So, Private VPC or on-premises resources.

115
00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:19.430
And so therefore to monitor the

116
00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:21.190
health of an EC2 Instance, what

117
00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:22.260
you can do is that you can create

118
00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:24.290
a CloudWatch Metric then you associate

119
00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:26.310
a CloudWatch Alarm with it and then

120
00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:28.391
you base the Health Check to monitor

121
00:04:28.391 --> 00:04:30.780
the CloudWatch Alarm itself and this

122
00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:32.710
is how you would effectively monitor

123
00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:36.160
the health of an internal resource.

124
00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:38.200
So let's take an example of a

125
00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:40.610
Solution Architecture for multi-region

126
00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:41.888
failover with RDS. So we have a

127
00:04:41.888 --> 00:04:44.470
main database and we have an Async

128
00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:46.360
replication into a different region,

129
00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:49.510
so we have us-east-1 to us-west-2.

130
00:04:49.510 --> 00:04:52.090
Then we have two ways, either we set up an

131
00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:54.027
EC2 Instance to monitor the health of the

132
00:04:54.027 --> 00:04:56.641
database and to expose a HFTP

133
00:04:56.641 --> 00:04:59.550
call /health-db route and that

134
00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:01.470
is one way of doing it. Or option two

135
00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:03.350
which I like more, is to define a CloudWatch

136
00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:05.180
Alarm and link the CloudWatch

137
00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:07.330
Alarm into a Health Check. Once the

138
00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:09.360
Health Check is off, we can link

139
00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:11.390
it to a CloudWatch Alarm as well.

140
00:05:11.390 --> 00:05:13.160
This is going to be linked to an

141
00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:15.130
SNS Topic or an CloudWatch Event

142
00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:16.430
which is going to trigger a Lambda

143
00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:17.980
function and then the Lambda function

144
00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:20.470
will update the DNS of your application

145
00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:22.860
into Route 53 and then promote

146
00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:25.360
your Read Replica in us-west-2

147
00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:27.441
and effectively you have achieved

148
00:05:27.441 --> 00:05:29.930
automated failover using Health

149
00:05:29.930 --> 00:05:32.200
Checks and Route 53. So, I hope

150
00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:34.050
that was helpful. I hope you liked this lecture.

151
00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:36.000
And I will see you in the next lecture.

